1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:01,780
Analytics runs on columns.

2
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:04,980
Rename one column upstream
and nothing crashes.

3
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:09,600
The queries below it keep running, quietly
returning wrong numbers, and nobody

4
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:10,900
notices for days.

5
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:12,740
That is schema drift.

6
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:15,380
DataHub is the open source
catalog underneath.

7
00:00:15,675 --> 00:00:20,175
Our agent reads it through MCP, the
standard way an agent calls a tool server.

8
00:00:20,395 --> 00:00:22,175
Four capabilities do the work:

9
00:00:22,235 --> 00:00:26,975
schema metadata, column-level lineage,
incidents, and catalog write-back.

10
00:00:27,275 --> 00:00:28,975
So let's cause it on purpose.

11
00:00:29,310 --> 00:00:33,730
One click renames order_placed_at to
order_created_at in the live catalog.

12
00:00:34,030 --> 00:00:36,210
This is the real catalog, not a copy.

13
00:00:36,350 --> 00:00:39,730
The drift is live, and
nobody downstream was told.

14
00:00:39,870 --> 00:00:41,890
Capability one: schema metadata.

15
00:00:42,255 --> 00:00:46,275
The agent reads the live schema from DataHub
and compares it with the committed baseline.

16
00:00:46,575 --> 00:00:47,635
One row differs.

17
00:00:47,695 --> 00:00:50,015
Same type, same position, new name.

18
00:00:50,015 --> 00:00:51,315
It infers a rename.

19
00:00:51,615 --> 00:00:53,235
Now watch the agent work.

20
00:00:53,375 --> 00:00:56,195
Each chip on the right is
a real call the agent made.

21
00:00:56,250 --> 00:00:58,810
The ones labelled datahub go through MCP.

22
00:00:58,810 --> 00:01:00,190
Search, to find the table.

23
00:01:00,330 --> 00:01:03,150
List schema fields, for
its exact column names.

24
00:01:03,290 --> 00:01:05,790
Get lineage, for the
changed column, downstream.

25
00:01:06,170 --> 00:01:10,590
Then get lineage paths between,
four times, to confirm each path.

26
00:01:10,905 --> 00:01:14,045
Capability two, the one that
matters: column-level lineage.

27
00:01:14,435 --> 00:01:17,965
DataHub records which downstream
column each column feeds.

28
00:01:18,025 --> 00:01:22,445
So the agent asks what reads this
column, not what touches this table.

29
00:01:22,745 --> 00:01:24,210
Three answers, not two.

30
00:01:24,370 --> 00:01:27,670
Three assets, in amber, read the
changed column and must be patched.

31
00:01:27,890 --> 00:01:32,370
Two, in blue, are downstream but read
order_date, a copy renamed one hop up,

32
00:01:32,370 --> 00:01:33,430
so they need nothing.

33
00:01:33,650 --> 00:01:35,750
Seven, in grey, are correctly untouched.

34
00:01:35,970 --> 00:01:37,430
Every skip carries evidence.

35
00:01:37,815 --> 00:01:41,975
This model really is downstream, but it
reads columns like order ID, quantity

36
00:01:41,975 --> 00:01:42,955
and unit price.

37
00:01:43,175 --> 00:01:45,355
None is on the changed column's path.

38
00:01:45,575 --> 00:01:47,755
Listing everything downstream is easy.

39
00:01:47,815 --> 00:01:49,815
Knowing what actually breaks is not.

40
00:01:49,815 --> 00:01:51,810
The language model never writes code.

41
00:01:52,050 --> 00:01:56,690
Patches come from sqlglot, which
edits the query's syntax tree, so only

42
00:01:56,690 --> 00:01:58,310
the changed tokens move.

43
00:01:58,450 --> 00:02:00,790
Four files across those three assets.

44
00:02:01,090 --> 00:02:02,870
The model's job is the explanation.

45
00:02:03,250 --> 00:02:04,425
Then a hard gate.

46
00:02:04,665 --> 00:02:06,605
Every column reference must resolve.

47
00:02:06,838 --> 00:02:08,265
23 of 23:

48
00:02:08,265 --> 00:02:12,745
15 against live DataHub schemas, 6
against the repaired schema this change

49
00:02:12,745 --> 00:02:15,325
creates, and 2 inside the query itself.

50
00:02:15,705 --> 00:02:17,645
One failure blocks the pull request.

51
00:02:17,970 --> 00:02:22,230
It opens a real pull request, a proposed
code change for a human to review,

52
00:02:22,450 --> 00:02:26,550
carrying the lineage path from the
changed column to every file it touched.

53
00:02:26,850 --> 00:02:31,670
Capabilities three and four go back into
DataHub. Corrected fine-grained lineage.

54
00:02:31,885 --> 00:02:33,425
The repaired column documented.

55
00:02:33,885 --> 00:02:35,585
Tags on every repaired asset.

56
00:02:35,805 --> 00:02:39,425
An incident raised through the
incidents API and moved to triage.

57
00:02:39,485 --> 00:02:41,185
And the run itself recorded.

58
00:02:41,485 --> 00:02:45,585
The next engineer opens the catalog
and finds the answer already there.

59
00:02:45,984 --> 00:02:48,564
column-level lineage to
find what truly broke.

60
00:02:48,864 --> 00:02:50,784
Deterministic edits to fix it.

61
00:02:50,784 --> 00:02:53,844
And DataHub as the source
of truth at both ends.
